<h1><span data-field="title" data-format="default">string_letters</span></h1>
<p>With this function you can remove all characters that are not classed as letters. This is handy for preventing people from entering unwanted characters into a text entry (like entering &quot;#&quot; to force a new line).</p>
<p class="note"><span data-conref="../../../assets/snippets/Tag_note.hts"><span class="note">NOTE</span></span> This function only detects and returns the 26 letter English alphabet from A - Z (upper or lower case).</p>
<p> </p>
<h4>Syntax:</h4>
<p class="code">string_letters(string);</p>
<table>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th>Argument</th>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>string</td>
<td><span data-keyref="Type_String"><a target="_blank" href="../../GML_Overview/Data_Types.htm">String</a></span></td>
<td>The string to convert to letters.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p> </p>
<h4>Returns:</h4>
<p class="code"><span data-keyref="Type_String"><a target="_blank" href="../../GML_Overview/Data_Types.htm">String</a></span></p>
<p> </p>
<h4>Example:</h4>
<p class="code">username = string_letters(username);</p>
<p>The above code will set the &quot;username&quot; variable to only hold the <i>letter</i> characters of the original string (id the original string was &quot;MacSw33ny&quot; the returned string would be &quot;MacSwny&quot;).</p>
<p> </p>
<p> </p>
